WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:02.090
- I'm about to give a talk to the

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:05.000
Bama football team, national champs.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.063
I'm a national champ, feels natural.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.340
- [Gary] You got your perspective.

00:00:13.292 --> 00:00:15.578
(crowd cheering)

00:00:15.578 --> 00:00:16.411
I just wanna be happy.

00:00:16.411 --> 00:00:18.056
Don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:18.056 --> 00:00:21.546
(slow hip hop tempo)

00:00:21.546 --> 00:00:23.820
(audience applause)

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.290
Sup fellas?

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.970
Thank you for having me.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.363
I'm super honored.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.440
Look, I'm gonna set some framework.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.070
I think, when you come and speak,

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:34.540
when you do something like this,

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.200
my biggest agenda in here
is to bring you guys value.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.950
You guys are busy, you
could be doing something

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:40.610
with your time, I don't
need to be wasting it.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.100
So, what I'd really like
to do is create a framework

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.860
that allows us to do some Q and A,

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.310
so I can answer some direct questions,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.180
whatever is running through your mind.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:50.270
Let me give you a little context,

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:51.930
cause I know the far majority of you

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.440
have no idea who I am.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.670
I was born in the former Soviet Union.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.140
I came to the US when I was three.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.170
We moved to a studio apartment in Queens.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.770
I lived with eight family members

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:05.100
in a studio apartment slightly bigger

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.970
than the stage that I'm on now.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:08.040
My parents grinded.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.530
I didn't see my dad until I was like 14.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.168
My mom and I kinda put it together,

00:01:12.168 --> 00:01:14.370
you know, walking two miles, no car,

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:15.203
all that kind of stuff.

00:01:15.203 --> 00:01:17.350
So it was a real humble beginning.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.770
Eventually my dad became a
manager in a liquor store

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.210
in New Jersey, so we moved to Jersey.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.480
That's where my
entrepreneurial hustle started.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:29.540
When I was six, I was running
four or five lemonade stands.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.830
I convinced, tricked,
whatever you wanna call it,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.710
my friends in the neighborhood
to stand there all day.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:38.820
I would make signs and look
at cars and try to make money.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.670
Ripping flowers out of people's yards

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.700
and selling it to them, selling rocks,

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.734
baseball cards, lemonade,
washing cars, shoveling.

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:49.550
I was very unique for all the immigrants

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.270
that came from Russia, because
education was the way out.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.300
And for me, my DNA just
didn't pull me that direction.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.070
So, I was a D and F student growing up.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.630
But when I was 12, 13 years old,

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.200
I was making two, three
thousand dollars a weekend

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.600
because I was flinging baseball and

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:05.820
football and basketball cards.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.210
So, that's just who I was and now,

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.120
in this day and age, the
entrepreneur is cool.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.850
Like, that's what people wanna be.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:13.683
People wanna make that money.

00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:15.690
But when I was growing
up, when I was your age,

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.260
or at least half your age,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.370
the only route out was school.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:23.380
And so frankly, I was shit
on my whole life, right?

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.040
My friends' parents
didn't want me hanging out

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.310
because I was dragging their kids

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:29.930
into bad school, different things.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.400
There was just, it made no sense to me,

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.400
the thought that I was being so successful

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.110
off the school field, and yet,

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.430
that wasn't being viewed in any good way.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.243
And so, that was my life.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.210
I went to a tiny school called

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.250
Mount Ida College in Newton, Mass.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:47.772
I was in my freshman dorm room

00:02:47.772 --> 00:02:51.920
in September of '94, playing Madden '95.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.640
My friend rolls in, I
was dominating in Madden,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.130
using the old school team, using

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.030
Joe Namath and the '69
Jets, that's my squad.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.250
My man walks in, he goes,
"You gotta come see this."

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.610
I walk in and it was the
first time I saw the internet.

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:05.810
You know, you guys are youngsters,

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:06.700
you're not gonna remember this,

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.880
but some of the coaches,
some of us old timers,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:09.837
we remember the...

00:03:09.837 --> 00:03:11.100
(imitating dial up sounds)

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:11.993
That shit.

00:03:13.553 --> 00:03:15.720
And I sat there, watched it,

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.960
and within the first
five to seven minutes,

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.100
I could feel that I was looking

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.090
at something completely different.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.620
And I waited for an hour.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.900
This is how old school this was.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:25.820
I had to wait an hour before it was

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:28.420
my turn to go on the internet.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.170
I went on and within another 30 minutes,

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.240
I was on a bulletin board selling cards.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.358
And I realized my dream of opening up

00:03:36.358 --> 00:03:39.420
10 thousand liquor stores, wine shops,

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.256
across the country cause my goal

00:03:41.256 --> 00:03:44.140
and still as I sit here
today as a 42 year old man,

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.110
my goal is to buy the New YorK Jets.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:47.940
And the way I've thought about that

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.410
was I was gonna open up
10 thousand wine stores,

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.073
flip it, sell it, buy the Jets.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.440
In that moment, in September of '94,

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:57.273
I was like wait a minute.

00:03:57.273 --> 00:03:58.230
I don't need to do that.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.900
I'm gonna use this thing.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.880
And from that day on to here,

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.460
the internet has been the foundation

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:04.970
of a lot of things I've thought about.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:07.050
Now it's mature now,
but in the early days,

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:08.690
when I launched the first

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:10.370
E-commerce wine business in America,

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:13.450
called winelibrary.com,
people clowned on me,

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:16.470
told my dad he was an idiot
for letting his kid do this.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.160
People thought the internet was a fad.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.320
Forget about social media, or SnapChat,

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.890
the whole thing was a fad.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.303
And so, that's how I came up.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.560
In five years, I built
my dad's liquor store

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.100
from a three to a sixty
million dollar business,

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.320
I made it, everybody
thought I was a whiz kid,

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.270
I was in papers, TV, it was amazing,

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.090
and then YouTube came out.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:40.280
YouTube came out, I thought
it was gonna be big.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.110
I started a wine show on YouTube,

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:43.680
cause I wanted to educate
people about wine,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.990
and within the first six
months, it got real big.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:49.620
And then YouTube sold to
Google for two billion dollars.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.850
And when I saw that flip,
I kinda looked at it,

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:57.130
and in an article, it said
Angel Investor, Ron Conway,

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.820
made all this money on a 25
thousand dollar investment.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.761
And I said, I've been so
right about email and Google,

00:05:02.761 --> 00:05:07.370
and the internet, the next time
I feel it, I'm gonna invest.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:10.250
And so, in 2006, the first three companies

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:14.063
I invested in were Facebook,
Twitter, and Tumblr.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.189
Yeah, I'm super fucking rich.

00:05:17.189 --> 00:05:20.840
(audience laughter)

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.163
So, that worked.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:30.130
And then my brother was
graduating school in 2009,

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.300
and even though I'm rich and I made it,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.340
I did a good thing, I like the process.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:37.220
I like the game.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.600
I like being an entrepreneur.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.490
Like, some of you guys in
here love, love playing.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.160
Others are just cool with
it, it's your way out,

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.340
there's a million different variables.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:45.920
Just some people can't breath with it.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.220
I can't breath without the game.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:49.684
I can't breath without being busy.

00:05:49.684 --> 00:05:52.100
I can't breath without
working 15 hours a day.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.440
I can't breath without every
minute being scheduled.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.040
I woke up at 4:30 this morning,

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:58.830
went to Chicago, met with Kraft,

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.670
got on a plane here, now with you guys,

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:03.170
gonna drive to Atlanta
tonight cause I got a meeting.

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:04.630
Like just, in it, just in it.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:05.790
I'm gonna be in studio with

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:07.760
Sonny Digital till three a.m. tonight

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:09.580
and then seven a.m., be in a meeting,

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:13.224
so just in it, cause I love
my process, I love my game.

00:06:13.224 --> 00:06:15.890
And that is what I've
been spending more time on

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.240
in the last three, four, five years.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:18.970
That's the thing I think
I can bring value here.

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:20.490
Because you're all gonna
have different paths.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.180
The thing that I've become fascinated with

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.240
is where's your mind at, right?

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.480
To me, for example, I'm
gonna throw you something

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.430
I've been thinking about
on the flight down here.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:30.750
To me, the craziest thing is that

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:34.423
for most of you, your talent
is your vulnerability.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:37.950
And that like, plays with my head.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:41.980
The fact that your talent is
actually your vulnerability.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:43.320
Because it's been so given to you,

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:44.930
you don't even know any different.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:46.480
I know you're talented.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.440
Cause I can context how fast you can run,

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.460
what you can do, what you
can live, how you can play.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:52.080
But your talent is the vulnerability

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.210
and to me, how you get over
there and understand that.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:56.960
For me, what's super interesting is

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.080
on some straight facts,
for 80 percent of you,

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.763
this is the APEX of your life.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:05.730
You're a Bama ball player.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:08.820
Like, most of you are not
gonna be in the league.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:10.210
Most of you are definitely not gonna be

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:11.820
in the league for more than three years.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:12.960
Just math.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.350
And so, what do you do with all the

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:16.560
leverage you have right now?

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.290
To me, I think a lot about, when I talk to

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:19.453
some of the kids on DM and shit like that,

00:07:19.453 --> 00:07:23.620
I think about, are they really
leveraging their moment?

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:25.330
Do they understand, while they're the

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:26.700
big dog on campus right now,

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.620
that the kid sitting next
to them in chemistry class,

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:30.700
in six years, is gonna have

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:32.460
all the leverage and they're not?

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.470
And they're too cool to
say hello to somebody,

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:37.380
and that leverage can change
the course of their life?

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.440
And so for me, you know, when I walk here,

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.060
I got a few minutes with
the coach, he's like,

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:42.960
what are you gonna talk about?

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.530
The only thing that kept running
through my mouth was like,

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.740
who in here, which fringe,
special teams player,

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:49.080
which third string line backer,

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:50.940
who's gonna hear what I have to say?

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:54.600
Which is, this is the greatest
year to ever be alive.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.040
Coach, I, others, we didn't grow up

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:56.873
with this internet thing.

00:07:56.873 --> 00:07:58.970
The access and ability you have to do

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:00.880
whatever you want is extraordinary.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.670
It's almost uncomfortable.

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:07.410
But, and it was amazing, a
couple guys on my team are here,

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.470
we walked by and the big sign
in the hall said, no excuses.

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.070
I just got so happy.

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:15.006
Like to me, it's just 100
percent accountability.

00:08:15.006 --> 00:08:17.320
Like to me, this is all about happiness.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:19.030
Like life, happiness.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:23.430
To me, this is just, the easiest
way I know how to be happy

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:27.100
is to blame myself for
everything, all of it.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:29.183
And there's tactics, I
was talking to coach,

00:08:29.183 --> 00:08:31.750
about E.T., E.T.'s my guy.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:33.680
Coming through here like, he's like E.T.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:36.030
challenged them to be off
social media for 30 days.

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:38.068
I was like, why the fuck would he do that?

00:08:38.068 --> 00:08:39.150
(audience laughter)

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:40.950
Cause that's a tactic.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:42.172
Like, that's short term.

00:08:42.172 --> 00:08:43.340
Right?

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:45.200
Like that's cool, you checked that box,

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:46.650
but the reality is it's more

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.060
about controlling what's happening.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:51.280
To me, technology is
the great ammo for you.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.120
I hate when people talk about
millennials and this and that.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:55.839
Like for me, this is it.

00:08:55.839 --> 00:08:57.900
You guys are the best at this,

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:00.910
you understand it the
best, are the best version.

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:02.710
It's always the evolution.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.080
But do you know how to
make it work for you?

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:06.550
That's a mindset game.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:08.500
And so I think about this all the time.

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:13.211
I'm getting 10 thousand DM's
a day right now from kids.

00:09:13.211 --> 00:09:15.180
They're coming from so many angles.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.610
They don't have enough,
so they resonate with me.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:18.860
Like what I grew up with.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:20.350
They have too much and their parents

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:21.479
are taking care of everything.

00:09:21.479 --> 00:09:23.500
To me, they're trying
to find their balance

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.850
because there's weakness
in too little or too much.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:27.850
And so for me, every day
I'm walking through this

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:29.880
and I'm seeing it and I'm wondering,

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.530
what is going through their mind?

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:32.550
What are they thinking about?

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:35.020
Because to me, the greatest leverage...

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:36.700
What's crazy about this program,

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:39.060
in comparison to all the other places,

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.990
is the leverage you sit with right now,

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:43.510
actually carries through.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:45.150
Once you have that A on your patch,

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:46.700
like you can play that forever,

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.740
but I'm just watching kid after kid,

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.280
in programs that have similarities

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.010
or other things, not leveraging.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.270
As a matter of fact, this is actually,

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:54.820
I'm glad I get to tell this story,

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:56.323
cause this really hits home.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:00.270
Dean Miller was incredible player here.

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:01.510
Obviously, I was super excited

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:03.500
when he went top ten to the Jets.

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:05.700
Unfortunately, he got hurt.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.390
And he was out of the league
within two, three years, right?

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:12.230
Basically, never really
played in the NFL for real.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:13.440
Obviously got very fortunate because

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.890
he was a top ten pick to get some money,

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:17.900
but he was in New York.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:19.590
And I tell all these guys
when they come through,

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.850
cause all these guys know
once they become a Jet,

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:24.420
they can DM me and I'm gonna see them.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:25.970
I tell him, I'm like, you're in New York.

